WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.278
An AI agent calls a model in many turns

2
00:00:03.278 --> 00:00:05.660
with a tool call between turns.

3
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:08.188
vLLM keeps a request's KV cache

4
00:00:08.258 --> 00:00:10.225
only while the request runs

5
00:00:10.296 --> 00:00:12.544
so after the tool call the whole

6
00:00:12.614 --> 00:00:14.722
context may be computed again

7
00:00:14.722 --> 00:00:16.970
and other conversations cut in.

8
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:19.629
Continuum holds a conversation's cache

9
00:00:19.689 --> 00:00:21.937
for a time-to-live after a tool call

10
00:00:21.937 --> 00:00:24.549
and serves whole conversations first come

11
00:00:24.549 --> 00:00:25.400
first served.

12
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.220
Here is a small server of our own.

13
00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.309
It computes prompts at four

14
00:00:30.373 --> 00:00:31.983
thousand tokens a second

15
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:34.108
spends one second on each reply

16
00:00:34.108 --> 00:00:36.490
and caches fourteen thousand tokens.

17
00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.497
Six conversations arrive in

18
00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:39.909
the first six seconds

19
00:00:39.909 --> 00:00:41.812
each with three or four turns

20
00:00:41.812 --> 00:00:43.960
and a tool pause after each reply.

21
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:47.460
vLLM serves requests as they become ready

22
00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.380
and evicts the least recently used cache.

23
00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:52.749
Its mean conversation latency is

24
00:00:52.812 --> 00:00:54.768
thirty-three point one seconds

25
00:00:54.831 --> 00:00:57.102
and it recomputes fifty-one thousand

26
00:00:57.165 --> 00:00:58.490
seven hundred tokens.

27
00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:01.360
Serving the oldest conversation first lowers

28
00:01:01.418 --> 00:01:03.480
the mean to twenty-eight point four.

29
00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:05.990
Add a time-to-live of eight seconds

30
00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:08.150
and recomputation falls to eighteen

31
00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.890
thousand two hundred tokens

32
00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.230
and the mean to twenty-four point one.

33
00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:14.839
But a held cache is memory other

34
00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:16.321
requests cannot use

35
00:01:16.321 --> 00:01:18.580
so the time-to-live needs care.

36
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:22.014
The real task starts from unmodified

37
00:01:22.094 --> 00:01:23.759
vLLM on one H100 GPU

38
00:01:23.838 --> 00:01:26.058
and the agent may change how

39
00:01:26.137 --> 00:01:28.040
it schedules and caches.

40
00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:30.323
A run serves forty-eight coding

41
00:01:30.386 --> 00:01:32.880
conversations and one hundred fifty-five

42
00:01:32.942 --> 00:01:34.065
tool-calling ones

43
00:01:34.065 --> 00:01:36.310
each at a public and a hidden load.

44
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.473
To pass, in all four groups

45
00:01:38.538 --> 00:01:40.610
it must beat the human reference

46
00:01:40.675 --> 00:01:42.618
on the geometric mean latency

47
00:01:42.618 --> 00:01:44.043
and at the ninetieth

48
00:01:44.043 --> 00:01:46.828
ninety-fifth and ninety-ninth percentiles

49
00:01:46.828 --> 00:01:48.900
and solve nearly as many tasks.

50
00:01:49.480 --> 00:01:53.080
The score compares with plain vLLM instead.

51
00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.651
Each workload's speed-up becomes

52
00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.149
a latency score: zero at one times

53
00:01:58.149 --> 00:01:59.538
fifty at ten times

54
00:01:59.607 --> 00:02:01.620
and sixty-six point six seven

55
00:02:01.689 --> 00:02:03.910
at a hundred times, with no cap.

56
00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:07.220
The four FlashLib tasks use the same score.

57
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.667
No model passed, though ten of sixteen beat

58
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:12.200
the reference's score.

59
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:15.990
Claude Opus 5.5 rebuilt Continuum.

60
00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.574
It groups requests into conversations

61
00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:21.231
by their prompts, and sets the time-to-live

62
00:02:21.291 --> 00:02:22.740
from recent tool pauses.

63
00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:25.415
Yet its ninety-ninth percentile was four

64
00:02:25.473 --> 00:02:27.624
thousand nine hundred ninety seconds

65
00:02:27.682 --> 00:02:29.251
against the reference's one

66
00:02:29.309 --> 00:02:30.530
hundred eighty-three.

67
00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:33.280
The task page does not say why.

68
00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.281
But with forty-eight coding conversations

69
00:02:36.281 --> 00:02:38.554
that percentile is the slowest one

70
00:02:38.617 --> 00:02:40.511
so a single stuck conversation

71
00:02:40.574 --> 00:02:41.710
is enough to fail.

72
00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:44.805
So: hold the cache across a tool call

73
00:02:44.805 --> 00:02:47.585
serve conversations rather than requests

74
00:02:47.585 --> 00:02:50.431
and a pass needs the mean and three tails

75
00:02:50.431 --> 00:02:51.490
in four groups.

76
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.886
As an exercise, double our cache

77
00:02:53.948 --> 00:02:55.994
and see how much the time-to-live

78
00:02:56.056 --> 00:02:56.800
still helps.

79
00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.910
Next, FlashLib.
